WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.581 --> 00:00:02.610
(bright jingle)

00:00:02.610 --> 00:00:05.190
- Whoever made the tweet asking it

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:08.820
how many LTT backpacks would
fit in the trunk of a Tesla,

00:00:08.820 --> 00:00:09.990
or whatever the question was--

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.100
- Oh, I didn't see that, that's hilarious.

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.320
- Someone made that
tweet on the LTT handle.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.153
- Oh, that's really funny.

00:00:14.153 --> 00:00:15.660
- And it did it!

00:00:15.660 --> 00:00:18.030
It looked up the dimensions
of the LTT backpack,

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:18.863
it looked up the dimensions--
- Shut up.

00:00:18.863 --> 00:00:20.280
- Of the trunk and it figured it out.

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.750
- How the (beep) did it do that?

00:00:21.750 --> 00:00:22.950
- Ask it, let's do it live!

00:00:22.950 --> 00:00:24.330
- Because I thought the dimensions

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:27.270
for the backpack are in picture form.

00:00:27.270 --> 00:00:28.980
- [Luke] Searching.

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:29.813
Searching for that.

00:00:29.813 --> 00:00:30.646
Now it's searching

00:00:30.646 --> 00:00:32.058
for backpack dimensions.
- Shut up!

00:00:32.058 --> 00:00:33.680
Shut up!
- Look at this!

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:34.513
- Have different shapes and dimensions,

00:00:34.513 --> 00:00:37.155
based on some rough estimates
I will try to answer it.

00:00:37.155 --> 00:00:38.488
- That's insane!

00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:42.510
That's actually nuts.

00:00:42.510 --> 00:00:43.620
- Based on some videos

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:46.110
of the Model Y trunk, shut up!

00:00:46.110 --> 00:00:48.570
It can fit about five to seven
standard carryon suitcases

00:00:48.570 --> 00:00:50.400
which have similar
dimensions and capacity.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.680
Holy--
- Which is accurate.

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:55.800
That statement is real.
- (beep)

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.370
- That's crazy!

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:00.930
- Look at this.

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:02.160
How did this happen?

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.910
How did Bing, no offense,
Microsoft, but how did Bing

00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:09.030
just beat Google to the
punch so dramatically

00:01:09.030 --> 00:01:11.490
at something that's so important

00:01:11.490 --> 00:01:14.792
and so core to their business?

00:01:14.792 --> 00:01:16.710
Well, there's actually a
really good reason for it.

00:01:16.710 --> 00:01:20.160
So AI has been blowing up
lately, both in the news

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:22.920
and in real life applications
across a ton of industries.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:24.480
So, you know, years ago it was only

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:26.970
in relatively small things
like helping doctors

00:01:26.970 --> 00:01:30.240
detect cancer early using
advanced pattern recognition,

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:31.770
and then a little bit more over the years

00:01:31.770 --> 00:01:33.420
with things like autonomous vehicles.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.590
But now AI is everywhere.

00:01:34.590 --> 00:01:37.230
It's creating whole
original pieces of art.

00:01:37.230 --> 00:01:39.438
It's holding conversations
with humans all over the place.

00:01:39.438 --> 00:01:41.850
It seems like we've just arrived

00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:43.620
at the beginning of the AI age.

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.000
There's this chart that keeps popping up

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:46.140
that hits extra hard,

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:49.770
which is the time to
reach 100 million users.

00:01:49.770 --> 00:01:51.810
And you can see the faster
and faster adoption curves

00:01:51.810 --> 00:01:53.820
with these increasingly
disruptive new technologies.

00:01:53.820 --> 00:01:57.360
So the telephone took 75 years to hit

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.370
this milestone 100 million number.

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:00.510
Then the mobile phone

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:03.120
reached the same mark in just 16 years.

00:02:03.120 --> 00:02:05.310
Netflix took only 10 years.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:08.580
and Twitter took six and
Gmail only took five.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:12.013
Facebook in 48ish months
was absolutely massive.

00:02:12.013 --> 00:02:14.780
Instagram hit it in just 30 months.

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:18.630
Now TikTok we view as this
gigantic existential threat,

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:21.780
nine months to 100 million users.

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:24.150
ChatGPT, two months.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:26.580
I mean, looking at numbers
like that, like I buy it,

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:28.710
like it seems almost
obvious that we're clearly

00:02:28.710 --> 00:02:31.710
on the precipice of
something really, really big

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:32.940
that's gonna change everything.

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.830
So seeing Microsoft at the forefront of it

00:02:34.830 --> 00:02:37.800
with this new Bing shouldn't
really be a surprise.

00:02:37.800 --> 00:02:40.440
I mean, people are already
talking to these chatbots

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:41.880
and asking it all sorts of questions.

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:43.410
So it sort of feels natural

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:46.200
having this chatbot act as
your co-pilot for the web

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:47.460
alongside search

00:02:47.460 --> 00:02:50.250
instead of just a traditional
search engine full of links.

00:02:50.250 --> 00:02:51.330
Like, that sounds pretty sick.

00:02:51.330 --> 00:02:53.190
But there is one thing that's gonna follow

00:02:53.190 --> 00:02:55.918
this conversational AI
thing everywhere it goes,

00:02:55.918 --> 00:02:57.630
everywhere you see it,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:01.164
which is that sometimes it's just wrong.

00:03:01.164 --> 00:03:04.080
Sometimes it just says
things that aren't true

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:06.180
because fundamentally the AI doesn't know

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:08.910
if it's telling the truth or not.

00:03:08.910 --> 00:03:10.170
It doesn't understand that,

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:11.490
like that's not part of the model.

00:03:11.490 --> 00:03:13.740
Like what we're seeing
is it taking our inputs

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:16.830
and then creating outputs
based on related words

00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.630
that are most likely to go together.

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:20.760
It's not forming a
sentence like humans do,

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.740
it's generating a new sentence.

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.870
And so adding it to a
search engine like Bing,

00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.820
it's scraping all these relevant links

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.450
and information and
synthesizing new sentences

00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:31.680
just based on how it thinks

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:33.720
things should be pieced together.

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:38.070
It's not sentient, it doesn't
understand what it's saying

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:40.620
and so it's definitely
not fact checking itself.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.520
So we have to keep that

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:44.070
in the back of our mind
through all of this, right?

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:45.330
Every time you see a headline.

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.580
So it's really interesting with
these search engines, right?

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:49.140
Because on one hand you have Bing

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:52.620
who has everything to gain,
and then on the other hand

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:55.499
you have Google who
has everything to lose.

00:03:55.499 --> 00:03:57.960
I've had access to this
new Bing for a little bit.

00:03:57.960 --> 00:03:59.130
It's a limited preview

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:01.800
before they push it live
to the rest of the world.

00:04:01.800 --> 00:04:03.360
I've just been playing around with it.

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:05.280
Basically, it adds this chat experience

00:04:05.280 --> 00:04:07.170
alongside regular Bing.

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.850
It's essentially the same experience

00:04:08.850 --> 00:04:11.430
as talking to ChatGPT, but
instead of being limited

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:14.880
to a fixed data set that
cuts off at 2021, it'll pull

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:17.280
from the entire current web
that Bing can scrape from.

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:19.080
So like I said, you
can type in a question,

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:21.810
flip it over to chat, and it'll give you

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:24.720
a sort of nicely written
summary that's synthesized

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.030
based on what it finds
for similar queries.

00:04:27.030 --> 00:04:29.280
So if I ask it something kind of simple

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:33.840
like what's the average lifespan
of a cheetah in the wild?

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:34.680
Gives me an answer, right?

00:04:34.680 --> 00:04:36.990
It gives me a convincing
bunch of sentences.

00:04:36.990 --> 00:04:38.730
It actually gives me more
information than I ask for.

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:40.830
It tells me about
cheetahs and captivity too

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.200
which makes it, you know,
feel very convincing.

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.150
It also gives little
footnotes and citations

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:47.040
for some of its sources,
and it gives links

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:48.744
at the end if you want
to dig in some more.

00:04:48.744 --> 00:04:50.130
It's really impressive, actually.

00:04:50.130 --> 00:04:50.963
It looks good to me.

00:04:50.963 --> 00:04:52.800
This is like a real
product that's gonna ship

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.990
to all over the world,
like people everywhere

00:04:54.990 --> 00:04:56.880
in the next month or
two, I think they said.

00:04:56.880 --> 00:05:01.110
But this could only come
from Bing right now.

00:05:01.110 --> 00:05:02.670
Like, the more you use it

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.280
the natural language is
super, super impressive.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:07.380
The fact that it gives
me a convincing sounding

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:08.730
couple of sentences in a row

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:12.540
and strings it together based
on my input, super cool.

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.130
But the more you use it,

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:17.490
the more you start to
see these weird patterns

00:05:17.490 --> 00:05:20.880
and these habits and these shortcomings.

00:05:20.880 --> 00:05:24.270
Again, mostly in the fact that sometimes

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:25.110
it's just gonna be wrong.

00:05:25.110 --> 00:05:27.480
A little game I like to
play is ask it a question

00:05:27.480 --> 00:05:29.610
you already know the answer to

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:32.520
and then read what it
says and spot the error.

00:05:32.520 --> 00:05:33.840
So I asked it right now, okay,

00:05:33.840 --> 00:05:36.090
what are the best smartphone
cameras right now?

00:05:36.090 --> 00:05:39.245
And it gave me S23 Ultra, Pixel 7 Pro,

00:05:39.245 --> 00:05:41.010
and iPhone 14 Pro Max

00:05:41.010 --> 00:05:43.530
with this nice little writeup
with some specs for each.

00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:45.450
That's actually a pretty good list,

00:05:45.450 --> 00:05:47.700
but it is wrong about some
of these numbers here.

00:05:47.700 --> 00:05:50.910
The S23 Ultra has a 200 megapixel camera

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.370
and a 12 megapixel front facing camera.

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:55.530
But yeah, okay, it's mostly right.

00:05:55.530 --> 00:05:56.370
I then asked it,

00:05:56.370 --> 00:05:59.310
what are the five best electric
vehicles out right now?

00:05:59.310 --> 00:06:02.310
And it gives me some,
five reasonable options.

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:06.660
But I don't know any expert
that would put the Jaguar I-Pace

00:06:06.660 --> 00:06:10.020
on their list right now
and leave the Rivian off.

00:06:10.020 --> 00:06:11.430
Like, basically the answers that it gives

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:12.630
are really convincing to someone

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:16.230
who doesn't know anything
already about that subject.

00:06:16.230 --> 00:06:17.700
But if you are already an expert

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:18.990
in the subject that you ask it about,

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:21.000
then you'll find that
the answers are like,

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.180
C plus, maybe B plus sometimes at best.

00:06:24.180 --> 00:06:25.200
So now you see what's happening,

00:06:25.200 --> 00:06:28.740
like now suddenly when
you're asking ChatGPT or Bing

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:31.200
about a factual thing or
something you need help with,

00:06:31.200 --> 00:06:34.590
suddenly you also should
probably add these layers on top.

00:06:34.590 --> 00:06:37.140
Like, am I a complete newb

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.030
in this topic that I'm asking it about?

00:06:39.030 --> 00:06:41.340
Am I just willing to blindly trust

00:06:41.340 --> 00:06:44.520
whatever this spits out
without any further research?

00:06:44.520 --> 00:06:48.450
Is a B plus answer gonna
be good enough for me

00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:51.063
even if it might have some
possible errors in it?

00:06:52.110 --> 00:06:54.600
You know, that might be good
enough for just asking like,

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:56.610
you know, how old a cheetah
gets or something like that

00:06:56.610 --> 00:06:57.810
but maybe not good enough

00:06:57.810 --> 00:07:00.780
for planning a trip or meal planning

00:07:00.780 --> 00:07:02.670
for someone with an allergy
or something like that.

00:07:02.670 --> 00:07:03.870
And then if you look around the internet,

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:05.520
people have gotten it to give

00:07:05.520 --> 00:07:09.090
increasingly more and more
unhinged answers over time

00:07:09.090 --> 00:07:11.310
as it tries to simulate conversations

00:07:11.310 --> 00:07:13.560
and stay in the flow
with natural language.

00:07:13.560 --> 00:07:15.330
I've seen anywhere from arguing

00:07:15.330 --> 00:07:18.810
about simple corrections
to spewing weird stories

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:20.730
about how it's spied on its own developers

00:07:20.730 --> 00:07:22.530
or how it wants to be sentient,

00:07:22.530 --> 00:07:24.360
to gaslighting people about things

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:26.250
and lying about its previous answers

00:07:26.250 --> 00:07:28.620
and just saying some
straight up scary stuff.

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.790
Just go to the Bing subreddit

00:07:29.790 --> 00:07:31.050
for like, an all you can eat helping

00:07:31.050 --> 00:07:33.870
of all the insane stuff that Bing has said

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:35.732
to just the people testing it
over the past couple weeks.

00:07:35.732 --> 00:07:40.440
Like, can you imagine, can you
imagine if Google did this,

00:07:40.440 --> 00:07:43.500
if Google search, at the
top of search for people

00:07:43.500 --> 00:07:47.370
was just spewing out random
stories and misinformation

00:07:47.370 --> 00:07:50.940
and like, all kinds of
insane, unhinged things.

00:07:50.940 --> 00:07:52.170
That would not fly.

00:07:52.170 --> 00:07:53.070
Now to be fair, this version

00:07:53.070 --> 00:07:55.590
of Bing isn't out yet
to the public, right?

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:58.110
So it is still a small
group testing phase,

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.240
but even with this, like
Microsoft knew that some of it

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:03.150
is gonna get out there
and potentially go viral.

00:08:03.150 --> 00:08:05.700
It feels like they even
basically programmed in

00:08:05.700 --> 00:08:09.330
lots of friendly emojis
to try to soften the blow.

00:08:09.330 --> 00:08:10.740
So when it knows it's giving an answer

00:08:10.740 --> 00:08:12.810
to maybe a more controversial topic

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:15.551
or something that it doesn't
have a super clear answer for,

00:08:15.551 --> 00:08:17.640
you might get a little
smiley face at the end

00:08:17.640 --> 00:08:20.580
just so you don't, you
know, take it too seriously.

00:08:20.580 --> 00:08:23.640
Also, literally as of today
when I'm testing this,

00:08:23.640 --> 00:08:26.757
it started completely
bailing on a lot of topics

00:08:26.757 --> 00:08:30.120
that might just be the
slightest bit existential

00:08:30.120 --> 00:08:30.953
or dangerous.

00:08:30.953 --> 00:08:31.950
It just says, hmm,

00:08:31.950 --> 00:08:33.960
I prefer not to continue
this conversation.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:35.070
And then it just stops.

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:36.810
Just refuses to answer any more questions

00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:38.730
on that topic until you reset it.

00:08:38.730 --> 00:08:40.260
Which seems like a pretty good failsafe.

00:08:40.260 --> 00:08:41.610
It's a pretty good idea in hindsight.

00:08:41.610 --> 00:08:43.980
But we've already seen the other stuff.

00:08:43.980 --> 00:08:45.900
It's gotten out there,
the damage has been done.

00:08:45.900 --> 00:08:47.688
Like, the point still stands,

00:08:47.688 --> 00:08:49.920
this could have only come from Bing.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:52.620
Like, a lot of people might
have forgotten about this

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:54.300
or might not have even known about this,

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:56.100
but Google has been working

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.860
on conversational AI stuff for years.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.330
We've seen Google Assistant.

00:09:00.330 --> 00:09:04.440
But they also literally
showed an AI chatbot demo

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:07.890
on stage at Google IO in 2021

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.740
where you could have this whole
conversation with any person

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:12.685
or object or anything in the
universe that you wanted.

00:09:12.685 --> 00:09:17.010
Their demo on stage was
asking Pluto about itself,

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.180
nice and friendly, right?

00:09:18.180 --> 00:09:20.220
Oh, what's it like to be you, Pluto?

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:22.710
What would it feel like if I visited you?

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:24.630
How do you feel so far from the sun?

00:09:24.630 --> 00:09:25.463
The difference

00:09:25.463 --> 00:09:28.350
with Google is this was
never shipped as a product.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:30.210
Like, this was an
internal research project.

00:09:30.210 --> 00:09:34.470
But the idea of displacing
their massive search

00:09:34.470 --> 00:09:36.780
and ads business with a chatbot

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:39.903
that gets things wrong
all the time is insane.

00:09:40.890 --> 00:09:42.060
It can't happen, right?

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.520
So literally, search and
ads is more than half

00:09:44.520 --> 00:09:47.340
of Google's revenue as a company.

00:09:47.340 --> 00:09:49.500
That's what having everything
to lose looks like.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:53.130
Now, to be fair, Google
did hold an event in Paris

00:09:53.130 --> 00:09:55.350
the day after Microsoft's event,

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:56.700
which was them talking a little more

00:09:56.700 --> 00:10:01.200
about their chat with search
AI plans, and they did say

00:10:01.200 --> 00:10:02.033
they're planning

00:10:02.033 --> 00:10:05.160
on eventually doing a chat
bot on top of Google search.

00:10:05.160 --> 00:10:06.060
It's called Bard.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:08.610
It was much more subdued though, and yes,

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:11.790
it also literally did
have a factual mistake

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.080
in the promo for it.

00:10:13.080 --> 00:10:14.880
So look, I actually like the idea,

00:10:14.880 --> 00:10:16.747
I obviously think it's smart

00:10:16.747 --> 00:10:19.740
when you're on the precipice
of this huge AI thing

00:10:19.740 --> 00:10:22.620
to have AI kind of be
this co-pilot for the web,

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:24.300
to help you around the internet.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:27.510
The idea of it summarizing a longer piece

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:30.450
into some bullet points accurately
is, that would be great.

00:10:30.450 --> 00:10:32.220
Like the fact that it
could give you sparknotes

00:10:32.220 --> 00:10:34.410
for a longer book you
haven't read yet, cool.

00:10:34.410 --> 00:10:37.530
It could even help you meal
plan, help you plan a trip,

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:39.030
help you make a purchase decision.

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.040
But it's clear that we're
still at the beginning of this.

00:10:41.040 --> 00:10:43.620
Like, there are so many
unanswered questions

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:46.590
from obviously the fact checking to like,

00:10:46.590 --> 00:10:51.120
do schools embrace this or ban this?

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:55.020
Or like, how do search
engines keep sending traffic

00:10:55.020 --> 00:10:58.410
to the publishers who are the sources

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:00.270
that the chatbot is scraping from?

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:01.500
I mean, you get the links at the bottom

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.270
but a lot of people are not
gonna click those anymore

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.190
if you just give 'em the answer
above the search results.

00:11:05.190 --> 00:11:06.900
So right now, in its current stage

00:11:06.900 --> 00:11:10.590
my take is anything we do
with any of these AI tools

00:11:10.590 --> 00:11:14.580
should be a collaboration
with the human touch.

00:11:14.580 --> 00:11:18.120
Like, you wouldn't just
put in a query in DALL-E

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.070
and then just take whatever it generates

00:11:20.070 --> 00:11:22.770
and put it in a frame and
just call that art, right?

00:11:22.770 --> 00:11:26.040
It's more for inspiration for
your own paint and canvas.

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:28.440
Like you wouldn't ask
ChatGPT to write an essay

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:31.410
and then just copy and paste
it and submit it as your own.

00:11:31.410 --> 00:11:33.840
It's supposed to be the
inspiration for the framework

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:36.354
for your own piece, for the human touch.

00:11:36.354 --> 00:11:39.420
So of course you shouldn't
ask the Bing chatbot

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:41.250
what TV you should buy and then just like,

00:11:41.250 --> 00:11:44.010
mindlessly click and buy
the first one that comes up.

00:11:44.010 --> 00:11:45.090
I mean, it could be fine

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.910
but it could also be a C plus answer.

00:11:47.910 --> 00:11:50.040
You should use that as a springboard

00:11:50.040 --> 00:11:51.900
for your own more informed research,

00:11:51.900 --> 00:11:55.230
especially on topics that you
don't already know much about.

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:59.310
Like, maybe don't just buy
19 backpacks immediately

00:11:59.310 --> 00:12:01.320
when asking if it can fit
in the back of a Tesla.

00:12:01.320 --> 00:12:03.506
Maybe check its work first.

00:12:03.506 --> 00:12:05.340
Thanks for watching.

00:12:05.340 --> 00:12:07.087
Catch you the next one.

00:12:07.087 --> 00:12:08.709
Peace.

00:12:08.709 --> 00:12:11.376
(playful music)

